import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"What Rho-core earns on a real research task","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/core-value-episode.md","filePath":"records/core-value-episode.md"}'),n={name:"records/core-value-episode.md"};function i(s,e,d,c,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="what-rho-core-earns-on-a-real-research-task" tabindex="-1">What Rho-core earns on a real research task <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-rho-core-earns-on-a-real-research-task" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What Rho-core earns on a real research task&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Updated: 2026-10-29. Full evidence: <code>work/core-comparison-20261029/</code> (protocol, harness, raw records, two agent arms).</p><h2 id="why-this-was-run" tabindex="-1">Why this was run <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-was-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why this was run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rho&#39;s managed action path uses Core&#39;s public CLI and registers one executor (R) and one action (<code>r.execute</code>, which runs a user-supplied script). Agent native tools are a separate path; the experiment explicitly required its managed arm to use Core. That made it worth asking whether Core&#39;s execution abstraction helps real research work or merely constrains it. The question was put as a controlled comparison, with &quot;Core is not needed here&quot; allowed as an outcome.</p><p>The task reused the existing scientific pilot (Iris sepal length, exact label permutation, <em>k</em> = 10/15/16 observations per species), whose answers are independently derivable by subset-sum DP. Every arm was graded against that independent answer, never against its own output.</p><h2 id="what-was-measured" tabindex="-1">What was measured <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-was-measured" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What was measured&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Three execution channels, same worker code and data:</p><ul><li>plain foreground tools; a 30-line shell convention (<code>mkdir</code> guard, detached child, stdout log, exit file); the resident Core Host through its public CLI.</li></ul><p>All nine scripted runs matched the independent answer on every field; no scientific difference was observed in these runs. What differed:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>at k=15 (~37 s of work)</th><th>wall time</th><th>protocol bytes</th><th>caller commands</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>plain tools</td><td>36.9 s</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>30-line convention</td><td>38.8 s</td><td>68,617</td><td>41</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>42.6 s</td><td>77,394</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><strong>The measured Core run was slower.</strong> 42.4 s against 36.9 s was not explained by <code>python -u</code> alone (a plain <code>-u</code> run took 37.6 s). The k=16 run wrote exactly 4,096 blobs, 4,079 under 100 bytes, matching the 44-byte progress lines. This identifies output spooling as a candidate cost; no coalesced-output comparison or release-build benchmark isolated its share.</li><li><strong>That also hit the retention limit</strong>: <code>observed 263454</code>, <code>retained 181141</code>, <code>truncated true</code>, with <code>retention_error</code> naming the chunk cap. The loss was reported — but a client reading <code>more_retained</code> alone (mine did) reads a truncated log as complete.</li><li><strong>Faults.</strong> Two concurrent identical intents: plain tools ran it twice (the second died on its own guard), the convention and Core dispatched once. Waiting caller killed 8 s in: plain tools lost the work; convention and Core kept it, one recovery command each. Owner killed with -9: plain tools had nothing, the convention reported <code>status=running pid=… alive=no</code> (contradictory), Core reported <code>Gone{evidence: MainPidAbsent, group_released: true}</code>, kept the record and did not re-dispatch.</li></ul><p>The convention reproduced survival and single dispatch in these fault cases. That does not establish equivalent durability, conflicting-request handling or crash-window guarantees. It did not produce the evidence-carrying outcome fact on which Rho&#39;s status mapping depends.</p><h2 id="two-agents-same-task" tabindex="-1">Two agents, same task <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-agents-same-task" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two agents, same task&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Same model, same directory, same question and output contract. The plain arm used ordinary tools; the managed arm had to compute through a <code>python</code>-only Host.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>plain</th><th>managed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>commands</td><td>10</td><td>29</td></tr><tr><td>wall time</td><td>~35 min</td><td>~60 min</td></tr><tr><td>computation for the answer</td><td>1.7 s (C, two independent traversals)</td><td>46.6 s (Python)</td></tr><tr><td>answer</td><td>exact</td><td>exact</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The plain arm compiled a C enumerator; the managed arm chose Python. The original report incorrectly attributed this difference to an inability to run compiled programs. A Python executor can invoke a compiler and its output through <code>subprocess</code>; a check with the same debug binary, a disposable Host and only a Python executor did exactly that, with exit 0 and retained child output. The R plugin likewise runs arbitrary user R scripts, prefixed with <code>.libPaths</code>; its action catalog is not a catalog of permitted scientific methods.</p><p>The observed ratios describe these runs, not a forced language restriction or a causal estimate of Core&#39;s cost. Neither fault landed: the native computation finished before 8 s, while no managed client waited long enough to trigger it. The agent arms therefore supply no comparison of recovery behavior.</p><p>One managed run of seven was also lost because Core executes a content-addressed <em>copy</em>: inside the run <code>__file__</code> points at <code>.rho/blobs/sha256/…</code> and <code>Path(__file__).parent / &quot;data.csv&quot;</code> fails, while <code>cwd</code> is the project. Verified directly. Snapshotting is defensible; making the snapshot the code&#39;s working identity is a small redesign of the agent&#39;s program.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Keep</strong>: activity observation (it has a consumer — <code>agent.activity.read</code>, <code>capture</code>, <code>outputs</code>); the honest-fact model for operations the application records; identity-keyed dispatch where an operation record already exists.</li><li><strong>Simplify</strong>: coalesce output spooling into larger blobs; make truncation impossible to misread; stop making the managed path the only path for agent computation; either make the script&#39;s path identity ordinary or say plainly in the contract that <code>__file__</code> is a blob.</li><li><strong>Drop</strong>: the requirement that agent computation routes through Core by default. The managed arm used 2.9× the commands, 1.7× the wall time and a 28× slower implementation in this comparison. Those observations warrant examining the interface and guidance; they do not prove Core required that implementation. Native tools are the normal path; select managed execution for a concrete need.</li></ul><h2 id="correction-and-follow-up" tabindex="-1">Correction and follow-up <a class="header-anchor" href="#correction-and-follow-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Correction and follow-up&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On 2026-10-29 the report&#39;s claims about compiled programs, arbitrary R, equivalent guarantees and performance causality were corrected. Original measurements and the prewritten protocol are retained; they were not rerun or regraded. The user selected removal of mandatory managed execution from code, documentation and development constraints, retaining activity observation and honest facts.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One scenario, one model, one run per agent arm; it shows mechanisms and costs, not rates. The Core binary was the local debug build of 2026-10-26, later only by the Windows port. The managed arm used a <code>python</code> executor I registered, whereas Rho&#39;s managed entry selects R. Both interpreters can launch other programs; the product was not measured, and no ordering of their costs follows from that choice. Nothing here speaks to irreducibly long analyses (MCMC, alignment, large model fits) — Core&#39;s survival guarantees are most plausible exactly there, and should be kept cheap enough to be worth having when they are needed.</p>',25)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
